     1	VALIDATED
     2	
     3	# Proof report
     4	
     5	## What is proven
     6	
     7	Under the supplied `MPY` semantics, the exact `how_many_times` closure in
     8	`solution.mpy` has the following partial-correctness property:
     9	
    10	> For every semantic string `str(S)` and substring `str(P)`, if the call
    11	> terminates, its result is `overlapCount(S, P)`.
    12	
    13	`overlapCount` tests the pattern at every successive suffix, so matches may
    14	overlap.  The empty pattern has `isLen(S) + 1` matches, one at every boundary.
    15	The theorem covers arbitrary `IntSeq` values, not only the concrete ASCII
    16	literals accepted by the supplied literal loader.
    17	
    18	The theorem executes the pinned closure body, name lookup, argument binding,
    19	conditionals, `len`, `startswith`, slicing, assignments, loop control, return,
    20	and frame pop through the supplied semantics.  The observable result is
    21	constrained by the `ensures` clause.  The entry configuration also fixes the
    22	global binding, builtins scope, environment, heap, stack, return state,
    23	exception state, and exit code.
    24	
    25	This is partial correctness.  `kprove` does not establish termination, although
    26	the source loop removes one code point from a nonempty string on each iteration.
    27	
    28	## Formal claims and validation scope
    29	
    30	- Program boundary: the exact closure invocation in `SPEC.target`, including
    31	  the body transliterated in `solution.mpy`.
    32	- Input domain: two values of the semantic string form `str(IntSeq)`.  Python
    33	  calls with non-string values are outside the theorem.
    34	- Observable final state: the returned integer.  The entry claim also preserves
    35	  all explicitly listed nonlocal operational cells.
    36	- Intended property: the number of starting positions at which `substring`
    37	  equals a contiguous part of `string`, with overlapping starts counted.
    38	- `SPEC.loop-inv`: for a nonempty pattern, starting with remaining suffix `S`
    39	  and count `C`, the exact loop terminates with an empty suffix and count
    40	  `C + overlapCount(S, P)`.
    41	- `SPEC.target`: the exact function call returns `overlapCount(S, P)`.
    42	
    43	The loop obligations are:
    44	
    45	1. Base: an empty remaining suffix exits immediately and
    46	   `overlapCount(.IntSeq, P) = 0` for nonempty `P`.
    47	2. Step: the real `startswith` result contributes either zero or one; the real
    48	   `[1:]` operation yields the next suffix; the circularity applies there.
    49	3. Entry: the empty-pattern branch returns `isLen(S) + 1`; otherwise the
    50	   initialized count `0` instantiates the loop claim.
    51	
    52	## Proof-extension inventory
    53	
    54	### `tailIS`
    55	
    56	- Class: definitional summary.
    57	- Semantic role: names the algebraic tail of an `IntSeq`; it does not replace
    58	  program execution.
    59	- Domain: all `IntSeq` values.  The `.IntSeq` and `iCons` equations are
    60	  disjoint, exhaustive, and structurally terminating.
    61	- Matched context and justification scope: a pure `tailIS(S)` term in any
    62	  context; its two equations cover the complete free-algebra domain.
    63	- State footprint: none.
    64	- Value influence: the recursive argument of `overlapCount` and the right side
    65	  of the slice lemma.
    66	- Value justification: constructor equations
    67	  `tailIS(.IntSeq) = .IntSeq` and
    68	  `tailIS(iCons(_, CS)) = CS`.
    69	- Dependents: `overlapCount`, the slice lemma, `SPEC.loop-inv`, and
    70	  `SPEC.target`.
    71	- Validation: equation overlap/coverage audit and the slice evidence below.
    72	
    73	### `overlapCount`
    74	
    75	- Class: definitional summary.
    76	- Semantic role: defines the requested mathematical result; it does not
    77	  intercept a Python call or K-cell transition.
    78	- Domain: all pairs of `IntSeq`.
    79	- Cases: empty pattern; empty source with nonempty pattern; nonempty source and
    80	  nonempty pattern.
    81	- Coverage and overlap: the three guards are exhaustive and pairwise disjoint.
    82	- Descent: the recursive case uses `tailIS(S)` under `S =/= .IntSeq`, reducing
    83	  source length by one.
    84	- Matched context and justification scope: a pure
    85	  `overlapCount(S, P)` term in any context, over the complete stated domain.
    86	- State footprint: none.
    87	- Value influence: the loop post-state and final result.
    88	- Value justification: one contribution for `startsWith(P, S)`, then the count
    89	  over the next suffix; the empty-pattern case counts all `n + 1` boundaries.
    90	- Dependents: both claims.
    91	- Validation: formal loop proof, the prompt examples, and 1,905 exhaustive
    92	  differential cases against an independently written position/slice oracle.
    93	
    94	### `buildIS(... [1:] ...) => tailIS(S)` simplification
    95	
    96	- Class: derived lemma.
    97	- Semantic role: normalizes the pure value returned by the supplied slice
    98	  helper.  It does not rewrite the `<k>` cell, skip lookup, alter control, or
    99	  abstract state.
   100	- Complete domain: every nonempty `S:IntSeq`; the exact left side is
   101	  `buildIS(S, clampHi(1, isLen(S), 1), isLen(S), 1)`.
   102	- Matched context: any term context.  Because this is equality of a pure total
   103	  function, its justification is context-independent and covers every
   104	  continuation and configuration in which the term can occur.
   105	- State footprint: none read, written, preserved, or abstracted.
   106	- Value influence: the next loop suffix, and therefore later branches and the
   107	  returned count.
   108	- Value justification: let `n = isLen(S) >= 1`.  `clampHi(1, n, 1)` is `1`
   109	  (also when `n = 1`).  `buildIS` selects exactly indices `1` through `n - 1`;
   110	  by induction on `n - 1`, that sequence is precisely `tailIS(S)`.
   111	- Equation consistency: where the lemma overlaps the supplied `buildIS`
   112	  equations, both sides select the same sequence.  Its nonempty guard excludes
   113	  the only value without a tail.
   114	- Dependents: `SPEC.loop-inv`, hence `SPEC.target`.
   115	- Control validation: fixed LLVM execution and extended proof execution agree
